meta {
  name: 05 - Cache Hit (Punctuation Casing Variant)
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/documents/{{document_id}}/ask
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "question": " what kind of insurance do I need..."
  }
}

docs {
  Run AFTER "03 - Ask About Document" (same document_id, same underlying
  question, different casing/punctuation — lowercase, no trailing "?").
  
  This verifies DocumentQnaService's caching: the two questions normalize
  to the same cache key (via normalizeForCacheKey), so this request should
  be served from cache rather than triggering a new LLM/vector-search call.
  
  How to verify (response content alone isn't proof):
  1. Watch the Spring Boot application console while sending this request.
  2. The "Question: ... / Answer: ..." log line only prints on a genuine
     cache MISS (logInteraction runs inside the method body, which the
     caching proxy skips entirely on a hit).
  3. If nothing prints to the console for this request, the cache hit,
     which is the actual proof — not just that a plausible answer came back.
  
  Also worth noting: the response should still be answering the ORIGINAL
  document content correctly, since normalization only affects the cache
  key — the raw question text is still what's used for retrieval and
  generation, unaffected by this caching layer.
}
